course: dictionary
lesson: 12220
title: Words about winning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Conquering means taking control of something, or beating something hard. It comes from the verb conquer, which changes its ending: conquer, conquers, conquered, conquering. Look for the -ing ending in a sentence, such as «Ali is conquering her fear of the dark.» Here conquering means she is beating that fear, bit by bit.
explain: In history, conquering means taking control of a country or people by force, often in war. «The Normans were conquering England in 1066» tells you they took control of England by force. Context helps: words like army, battle or kingdom are clues that the word is about war.
explain: Conquering can also be about effort. «Sam is conquering the big hill on his bike» means he is getting all the way to the top. Ask: is someone winning against something hard? If yes, conquering probably means overcoming it bravely.
explain: As an adjective, conquering describes someone who has won. «The conquering hero came home to a cheering crowd» means the hero, who had won, came home. Check by swapping in victorious: the hero came home victorious, and it still makes sense.
fact: Conquer is a verb: conquer, conquers, conquered, conquering.
fact: «Conquering» can mean taking control of a place or people by force, especially in war.
fact: «Conquering» can mean overcoming a difficult problem or fear by effort and bravery.
fact: «Conquering» can mean climbing a difficult mountain all the way to the top.
fact: As an adjective, «the conquering hero» means a winner, someone who is victorious.
exercise: «Ali is conquering her fear of the dark.» What does conquering mean here? | Making the fear bigger | Sleeping through the night | Running away from the fear for ever | *Overcoming the fear bravely
exercise: «The Romans were conquering new lands.» What does conquering mean here? | Painting maps of them | *Taking control of them by force | Visiting them on holiday | Cleaning them up
exercise: «The conquering hero came home to a cheering crowd.» What is the hero like? | Quiet and shy | *Victorious, having won | Lost and tired
test: «Sam felt proud after conquering the mountain.» What does conquering mean here? | Reading about mountains | *Climbing to the top of something hard | Falling down the slope | Forgetting all about the mountain
test: Which word means taking control of a country by force? | *Conquer | Sneeze | Whisper | Explore
exam: «Mia is conquering a difficult maths problem.» What does conquering mean here? | Copying the answer from a friend | Forgetting the problem completely | *Solving it with effort and patience | Falling asleep over it
